title = Branch policies, reviewers, and merge types in Azure Repos
mode = platform_theory

[diagram]

[code]
   branch: main (protected)
        │
        ├── require PR (no direct pushes)
        ├── min reviewers + optional path owners
        ├── build validation (pipeline must pass)
        ├── linked work items (traceability)
        └── merge types allowed (basic / squash / rebase+FF)
[/code]

[center][color=#0078d4]policy overrides[/color]  ·  inherit from project or set per branch[/center]

[description]

[b]Branch policies[/b] attach to a branch name pattern in Azure Repos: they can require pull requests, minimum reviewers, optional automatic reviewers for paths, successful builds from a specified YAML pipeline, and linked work items for traceability. Unlike informal team norms, policies are enforced server-side—attempts to bypass via force-push should fail when configured correctly.

[b]Merge strategies:[/b] basic merge, squash, and rebase with fast-forward map to Git outcomes similar to other hosts; pick defaults that match your release archaeology needs.

[b]Bypass permissions:[/b] emergency break-glass roles can bypass policies—treat as audited capability, not daily workflow.

[b]Cross-service linkage:[/b] requiring work items ties Git activity to Boards items—great for compliance, painful if developers forget IDs; provide templates and CLI helpers.

[b]Takeaway:[/b] treat policies as executable documentation: if the policy file does not match how the team really ships, either fix the process or simplify the policy to avoid shadow workflows.
